ir. So the user might be aiming for a hand with two pairs and three chows. But that's not enough for All Chows.\n\nAnother angle: the user's hand has a lot of suited tiles. Maybe they can form a Half Flush (6 points) if all tiles are in one suit and honors. But the hand has multiple suits. So Half Flush is not applicable.\n\nWhat about One Voided Suit (1 point)? If the user discards a tile from one suit, making two of the three suits void. For example, if they discard B7, which is Dots, then they have no Dots left except B4, B5, B6. Wait, B4, B5, B6 are Dots. So discarding B7 would leave them with Dots in B4, B5, B6. So not void. To have one voided suit, they need to have no tiles in one suit. The current hand has W (Characters), T (Bamboos), B (Dots), and F (Wind). If the user discards all tiles of one suit, but that's not possible here. For example, if they discard all W tiles, but they have W3, W6, W9. So not feasible. So One Voided Suit might not be applicable.\n\nConsidering all this, the best move is to discard a tile that is not part of any potential chows or pairs. The B7 is a single tile not part of any chow. So playing B7 would be the safest move. Alternatively, W9 is a terminal not part of any chow. So playing W9 could be another option. Between B7 and W9, which is safer?\n\nThe user has B4, B5, B6 for a chow. If they discard B7, they still have B4, B5, B6. So that's good. W9 is a terminal, but not part of any chow. So discarding W9 is safe. The user has a pair of T5s. The T5s are in the middle, so maybe they can form a chow with T4 and T6 if available. But the user doesn't have T4 or T6. So the T5s are just a pair.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to play B7, as it's a single tile not part of any chow or pair. Alternatively, W9. But the valid actions include B7 and W9. Let me check the unshown tiles. The unshown tiles have B7:4, so there are still four B7s left. So discarding B7 is safe. Similarly, W9 has 4 left. So either is safe. But which one is better for the hand's potential?\n\nIf the user discards B7, they can keep the B4, B5, B6 chow. If they discard W9, they can keep the W3, W6. The W3s are part of a pair. So maybe discarding W9 is better. But the user has a pair of T5s, which could be a pair. So the user might be aiming for a hand with two pairs and three chows. But that's not enough for All Chows. However, if they can form four chows and a pair, that would be All Chows. But given the current hand, it's not possible.\n\nIn conclusion, the best move is to discard B7, as it's a single tile not part of any chows or pairs, and it doesn't affect potential chows. So the answer is Play B7.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand contains potential for a concealed hand with two pairs and possible chows. Discarding a non-critical tile like B7 maintains flexibility for forming chows and pairs.  \nReason: B7 is a single tile not part of any chow or pair, making it the safest discard without disrupting potential patterns.  \nAnswer: Play B7"}
